// FLOWS
// redirect-same: Presentation — OpenID4VP redirect, same device. The user is on the verifier's website on the phone that holds the wallet. The page opens an eu-eaap:// link carrying a request_uri; the wallet fetches the signed Request Object, validates it, posts an encrypted response (direct_post.jwt) and opens the returned redirect_uri, whose response_code lets the browser session pick up the result. Frontend and backend are drawn as one Verifier; how the frontend picks up the result with the response_code follows the reference design in OpenID4VP §13.3.
//   1 Verifier: prepare transaction
//   1.1 Verifier: generate nonce
//   1.2 Verifier: ephemeral encryption key
//   1.3 Verifier: sign Request Object
//   2 Verifier → Wallet Unit: open eu-eaap:// link → AuthorizationRequest
//   3 Wallet Unit → Verifier: GET request_uri → RequestUriFetch
//   4 Verifier → Wallet Unit: signed Request Object → RequestObjectMessage
//   5 Wallet Unit: validate request, user consent
//   5.1 Wallet Unit: verify signature + access cert
//   5.2 Wallet Unit: validate registration cert
//   5.3 Wallet Unit: over-asking check
//   5.4 Wallet Unit: build KB-JWT, encrypt
//   6 Wallet Unit → Verifier: POST response_uri (direct_post.jwt) → ResponsePost
//   7 Verifier: validate response
//   7.1 Verifier: decrypt JWE
//   7.2 Verifier: match state
//   7.3 Verifier: vp_token vs DCQL
//   7.4 Verifier: issuer signature + x5c
//   7.5 Verifier: disclosures vs _sd
//   7.6 Verifier: KB-JWT nonce, aud, sd_hash
//   7.7 Verifier: exp / validity
//   7.8 Verifier: status list
//   8 Verifier → Wallet Unit: 200 redirect_uri → ResponseAck
//   9 Wallet Unit → Verifier: open redirect_uri
//   10 Verifier: frontend fetches result (response_code)
// dc-api: Presentation — OpenID4VP over the Digital Credentials API. The verifier's page calls navigator.credentials.get() with a signed OpenID4VP request (openid4vp-v1-signed). The browser and operating system invoke the wallet, on the same device or across devices via CTAP hybrid with a proximity check. The wallet checks the Origin against expected_origins and returns an encrypted response (dc_api.jwt) to the page; the Key Binding JWT is bound to origin:<Origin>, not to the client_id. Under the CIR both wallets and relying parties shall support this mechanism.
//   1 Verifier: prepare transaction
//   1.1 Verifier: generate nonce
//   1.2 Verifier: ephemeral encryption key
//   1.3 Verifier: sign Request Object
//   2 Verifier → Browser / OS: navigator.credentials.get() → DcApiRequest
//   3 Browser / OS → Wallet Unit: invoke wallet (local or CTAP hybrid)
//   4 Wallet Unit: validate request, Origin, consent
//   4.1 Wallet Unit: verify signature + access cert
//   4.2 Wallet Unit: validate registration cert
//   4.3 Wallet Unit: over-asking check
//   4.4 Wallet Unit: build KB-JWT, encrypt
//   5 Wallet Unit → Browser / OS: encrypted response (dc_api.jwt) → DcApiResponse
//   6 Browser / OS → Verifier: page forwards the response
//   7 Verifier: validate response
//   7.1 Verifier: decrypt JWE
//   7.2 Verifier: vp_token vs DCQL
//   7.3 Verifier: issuer signature + x5c
//   7.4 Verifier: disclosures vs _sd
//   7.5 Verifier: KB-JWT nonce, aud, sd_hash
//   7.6 Verifier: exp / validity
//   7.7 Verifier: status list
// redirect-cross: Presentation — OpenID4VP redirect, cross device. The verifier's website runs on a desktop; the user scans a QR code with the wallet on the phone. There is no redirect back, so the browser session cannot be bound to the response (session fixation, OpenID4VP §14.2). The CIR position: EUDI Wallets should not support the redirects-based mechanism for cross-device flows; HAIP recommends that verifiers use only same-device redirects. Cross device is meant to run over the Digital Credentials API.
//   1 Verifier: prepare transaction
//   1.1 Verifier: generate nonce
//   1.2 Verifier: ephemeral encryption key
//   1.3 Verifier: sign Request Object
//   2 Verifier → Wallet (phone): QR code scanned → CrossDeviceRedirect
//   3 Wallet (phone) → Verifier: GET request_uri → RequestUriFetch
//   4 Verifier → Wallet (phone): signed Request Object → RequestObjectMessage
//   5 Wallet (phone): validate request, user consent
//   5.1 Wallet (phone): verify signature + access cert
//   5.2 Wallet (phone): validate registration cert
//   5.3 Wallet (phone): over-asking check
//   5.4 Wallet (phone): build KB-JWT, encrypt
//   6 Wallet (phone) → Verifier: POST response_uri (direct_post.jwt) → ResponsePost
//   7 Verifier: validate response
//   7.1 Verifier: decrypt JWE
//   7.2 Verifier: match state
//   7.3 Verifier: vp_token vs DCQL
//   7.4 Verifier: issuer signature + x5c
//   7.5 Verifier: disclosures vs _sd
//   7.6 Verifier: KB-JWT nonce, aud, sd_hash
//   7.7 Verifier: exp / validity
//   7.8 Verifier: status list
//   8 Verifier → Wallet (phone): 200 {} → ResponseAck
//   9 Verifier: frontend polls for the result
// redirect-same-mdoc: Presentation — OpenID4VP redirect, same device, ISO mdoc. The same-device redirect flow, asking for the PID in ISO mdoc format (mso_mdoc, OpenID4VP Appendix B.2) instead of SD-JWT VC. The DCQL query names the doctype and addresses data elements as [namespace, element]. The vp_token carries a base64url CBOR DeviceResponse: issuer-signed data elements whose digests are signed in the Mobile Security Object, and a device signature over the SessionTranscript. There is no Key Binding JWT; the SessionTranscript binds the presentation to client_id, nonce, response_uri and the response encryption key (OpenID4VPHandover), so the Verifier rebuilds it from its own request. Under the CIR (Annex XII OIDFVP-HAIP-GEN-04) relying parties and wallets follow the HAIP ISO mdoc profile for this format.
//   1 Verifier: prepare transaction
//   1.1 Verifier: generate nonce
//   1.2 Verifier: ephemeral encryption key
//   1.3 Verifier: sign Request Object
//   2 Verifier → Wallet Unit: open eu-eaap:// link → AuthorizationRequest
//   3 Wallet Unit → Verifier: GET request_uri → RequestUriFetch
//   4 Verifier → Wallet Unit: signed Request Object → RequestObjectMessage
//   5 Wallet Unit: validate request, user consent
//   5.1 Wallet Unit: verify signature + access cert
//   5.2 Wallet Unit: validate registration cert
