/** SessionTranscript (rebuilt by the Verifier), produced by Wallet. */
export type SessionTranscript = [null /* 0: SessionTranscript */, null /* 1: SessionTranscript */, (string | bstr)[] /* 2: OpenID4VPHandover */]
/** OpenID4VPHandoverInfo (hashed into the handover), produced by Wallet. */
export type OpenID4VPHandoverInfo = [string /* 0: handover clientId */, string /* 1: handover nonce */, bstr /* 2: handover jwkThumbprint */, string /* 3: handover responseUri */]

// ── Verifier decrypts and validates the Authorization Response ── Verifier → Verifier · step 7 in every flow
// The Verifier takes the response apart from the outside in: the JWE envelope, the binding to its own transaction, the vp_token against the DCQL query, then each credential (issuer signature, the selectively disclosed claims, holder binding, validity, status). Holder binding is the Key Binding JWT for an SD-JWT VC and the device signature over the SessionTranscript for an mdoc. Every check runs on the Verifier itself and any failure rejects the whole response (OpenID4VP §8.6, §14.9). Below, each check lists the fields it looks at and the result each one must have.
// rule: Verifier: MUST · "Verifiers MUST validate the VP Token in the following manner: … If any of the checks pertaining to the VP Token or the overall response fails, the VP Token MUST be rejected." · "it MUST NOT rely on the Wallet to enforce these constraints. The Wallet is not controlled by the Verifier and the Verifier MUST perform its own security checks on the returned Credentials and Presentations."

// ── Verifier decrypts the JWE with the ephemeral private key ── Verifier → Verifier · step 7.1 in every flow
// The response parameter is an unsigned, encrypted JWT (OpenID4VP §8.3). The Verifier looks up the ephemeral key pair it generated for this transaction, runs the ECDH-ES key agreement with the Wallet's epk and decrypts. Nothing inside is trusted before this step succeeds.
// rule: Verifier: MUST · "The JWE enc (encryption algorithm) header parameter (see Section 4.1.2 of [RFC7516]) values A128GCM and A256GCM (as defined in Section 5.3 of [RFC7518]) MUST be supported by Verifiers."
// check alg (JWE header): ECDH-ES, the key agreement the Verifier offered. Any other value: reject.
// check enc (JWE header): A128GCM or A256GCM, and one of the values listed in client_metadata.encrypted_response_enc_values_supported.
// check kid (JWE header): Equals the kid of the ephemeral key in client_metadata.jwks of this transaction. Unknown kid: reject.
// check epk (JWE header): An EC public key on P-256 that is a valid point on the curve; it is the Wallet's half of the key agreement.
// check ciphertext + tag: Decrypts with the ephemeral private key and the authentication tag verifies. Failure means the response was altered or was not encrypted for this transaction.
// check payload: A JSON object with vp_token (and state where the request carried one). The JWE is not signed, so its content proves nothing yet; the checks below do.

// ── Verifier matches state to a recent Authorization Request ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 7.2, redirect-cross 7.2, redirect-same-mdoc 7.2
// In the redirect flows anyone can POST to the response_uri. state ties the POST to a transaction the Verifier actually started and is still waiting for (OpenID4VP §14.3.2). Over the Digital Credentials API the browser returns the response to the page that asked, so there is no state.
// rule: Verifier: SHOULD · "The Verifier SHOULD protect its Response URI from inadvertent requests by checking that the value of the received state parameter corresponds to a recent Authorization Request."
// check state: Present and byte-for-byte equal to the state of an open transaction created by this Verifier.
// check transaction age: The transaction is recent, i.e. inside the Verifier's timeout (a few minutes). Expired: reject.
// check transaction status: No response was accepted for this transaction yet. A second POST with the same state is a replay: reject.

// ── Verifier checks the vp_token structure against the DCQL query ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 7.3, dc-api 7.2, redirect-cross 7.3, redirect-same-mdoc 7.3
// The vp_token must answer the DCQL query of this transaction, no more and no less (OpenID4VP §8.1, §8.6, §6.4). This is a structural check; the cryptographic checks of each presentation follow.
// rule: Verifier: MUST · "Validate the format of the VP Token as defined in Section 8.1. … Validate that the returned Credential(s) meet all criteria defined in the query in the Authorization Request (e.g., Claims included in the presentation)." · "Check that the set of Presentations returned satisfies all requirements defined in the Verifier's request as described in Section 6.4."
// check vp_token: A JSON object. Each key is the id of a credential query from the request; each value is an array of presentations.
// check vp_token keys [except redirect-same-mdoc]: Only ids that were requested, and every required query (or one option of each required credential_set) is answered, e.g. pid-sd-jwt.
// check presentations per key: Exactly one, unless the query set multiple: true.
// check format [except redirect-same-mdoc]: Matches the query's format. For dc+sd-jwt: an SD-JWT with Disclosures and a Key Binding JWT, separated by ~.
// check vct [except redirect-same-mdoc]: The credential's vct is one of the query's meta.vct_values, e.g. urn:eudi:pid:de:1.
// check claims [except redirect-same-mdoc]: Every claim path the query asked for is disclosed (or one claim_sets option is satisfied).
// check vp_token keys [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Only ids that were requested, and every required query (or one option of each required credential_set) is answered, e.g. pid-mdoc.
// check format [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Matches the query's format mso_mdoc: each presentation is a base64url string that decodes to a CBOR DeviceResponse (OpenID4VP B.2.5), one per mdoc (HAIP §5.3.1).
// check DeviceResponse version + status [only redirect-same-mdoc]: version "1.0" and status 0 (OK); documents holds one document, without an errors entry.
// check docType [only redirect-same-mdoc]: The document's docType equals the query's meta.doctype_value, e.g. eu.europa.ec.eudi.pid.1 for the PID (CIR Annex I 4.1).
// check issuerSigned.nameSpaces [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Holds a data element for every requested [namespace, element] path, e.g. [eu.europa.ec.eudi.pid.1, family_name] (or one claim_sets option is satisfied), and none the query did not ask for.

// ── Verifier verifies the issuer signature and the x5c chain ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 7.4, dc-api 7.3, redirect-cross 7.4, redirect-same-mdoc 7.4
// Proves the credential comes from a trusted issuer and was not changed. The key comes from the end-entity certificate of the chain the credential carries (x5c in an SD-JWT VC, x5chain in the issuerAuth of an mdoc), and that chain is validated (RFC 9901 §7.1, SD-JWT VC §3.5, HAIP §6.1.1).
// rule: redirect-same, dc-api, redirect-cross: Verifier: MUST | redirect-same-mdoc: Verifier: MUST · "This specification mandates the support for X.509 certificate-based key resolution to validate the issuer signature of an SD-JWT VC. This MUST be supported by all entities (Issuer, Wallet, Verifier)." · "Validate the integrity and authenticity of the Presentation and Credential."
// check typ (JWT header) [except redirect-same-mdoc]: dc+sd-jwt.
// check alg (JWT header) [except redirect-same-mdoc]: An asymmetric algorithm the Verifier accepts, at least ES256. none: always reject.
// check x5c (JWT header) [except redirect-same-mdoc]: The chain validates up to a trust anchor the Verifier trusts for this credential type (e.g. the PID Provider trusted list); every certificate is within its validity period and not revoked.
// check signature [except redirect-same-mdoc]: Verifies over the Issuer-signed JWT with the public key of the x5c end-entity certificate.
// check iss [except redirect-same-mdoc]: Identifies the issuer and is consistent with the end-entity certificate, e.g. https://pid-provider.example.de.
// check issuerAuth [only redirect-same-mdoc]: A COSE_Sign1 [protected, unprotected, payload, signature] whose payload is the tag-24 encoded Mobile Security Object.
// check alg (protected header) [only redirect-same-mdoc]: An asymmetric algorithm the Verifier accepts, e.g. -7 (ES256); one of vp_formats_supported.mso_mdoc.issuerauth_alg_values when the request set it (OpenID4VP B.2.2).
// check x5chain (unprotected header) [only redirect-same-mdoc]: The chain validates up to a trust anchor the Verifier trusts for this docType (e.g. the PID Provider trusted list); every certificate is within its validity period and not revoked.
// check x5u + x5t (protected header) [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Present for the PID (CIR Annex I 4.1). x5t is [-16, SHA-256 hash] and equals the thumbprint of the signer certificate, the one in x5chain or fetched from x5u.
// check signature [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Verifies over the protected header and the MSO (COSE Sig_structure, RFC 9052) with the public key of the signer certificate.
// check MSO version + docType [only redirect-same-mdoc]: version "1.0"; the MSO's docType equals the document's docType.

// ── Verifier matches the Disclosures against the _sd digests ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 7.5, dc-api 7.4, redirect-cross 7.5
// Each Disclosure carries one selectively disclosed claim. Its digest must appear in the signed payload, so the issuer signature covers the claim even though the claim travels outside the JWT (RFC 9901 §7.1). The result is the processed payload with the disclosed claims put back in place.
// rule: Verifier: MUST · "Check that the _sd_alg claim value is understood and the hash algorithm is deemed secure according to the Holder or Verifier's policy … Calculate the digest over the base64url-encoded string as described in Section 4.2.3." · "If any digest value is encountered more than once in the Issuer-signed JWT payload (directly or recursively via other Disclosures), the SD-JWT MUST be rejected. … If any Disclosure was not referenced by digest value in the Issuer-signed JWT (directly or recursively via other Disclosures), the SD-JWT MUST be rejected."
// check _sd_alg: A hash algorithm the Verifier understands and considers secure; sha-256 when absent.
// check each Disclosure: base64url-decodes to a JSON array [salt, name, value] (or [salt, value] for an array element).
// check digest of each Disclosure: The hash over the base64url Disclosure string appears in an _sd array (or as {"...": digest} in an array) of the payload or of another Disclosure.
// check duplicates: No digest occurs twice, and every Disclosure is referenced. Otherwise: reject.
// check claim names: Not _sd or ..., and not already present in the same object of the payload.

// ── Verifier verifies the Key Binding JWT (cnf key, nonce, aud, iat, sd_hash) ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 7.6, dc-api 7.5, redirect-cross 7.6
// The Key Binding JWT proves that the holder of the key the issuer bound into the credential presented it, to this Verifier, in this transaction (RFC 9901 §7.3, OpenID4VP §14.1.2). It closes replay: a presentation copied from another transaction fails on nonce or aud.
// rule: Verifier: MUST · "EAAP-SD-JWT VC-01: If the SD-JWT VC EAA contains the cnf claim, the corresponding SD-JWT VC EAAP shall be a SD-JWT+KB as specified in IETF SD-JWT [2]."
// check KB-JWT: Present whenever the credential has a cnf key (HAIP §6.1.1.1).
// check typ (KB-JWT header): kb+jwt.
// check alg (KB-JWT header): ES256 or another algorithm the Verifier accepts (client_metadata.vp_formats_supported); never none.
// check signature: Verifies with the public key in the credential's cnf.jwk.
// check nonce: Equals the nonce of this transaction's Request Object.
// check aud [except dc-api]: Equals the client_id of the request, e.g. x509_hash:<hash of the access certificate> (OpenID4VP B.3.6).
// check iat: Within an acceptable window around now (a few minutes, plus clock skew).
// check sd_hash: Equals the base64url hash (_sd_alg) over the Issuer-signed JWT and the presented Disclosures, up to and including the last ~.
// check aud [only dc-api]: origin: followed by the web origin of the page that called the API, e.g. origin:https://verifier.example.eu (OpenID4VP B.3.6).

// ── Verifier checks exp and other validity claims ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 7.7, dc-api 7.6, redirect-cross 7.7, redirect-same-mdoc 7.7
// Checks the time window the issuer set: exp and nbf in an SD-JWT VC (RFC 9901 §7.1, RFC 7519 §4.1.4), validityInfo in the Mobile Security Object of an mdoc. A validity claim the Verifier's policy requires but the credential lacks rejects the credential.
// rule: Verifier: MUST · "Check that the SD-JWT is valid using claims such as nbf, exp, and aud in the processed payload, if present. If a required validity-controlling claim is missing (see Section 9.7), the SD-JWT MUST be rejected." [R9]
// check exp [except redirect-same-mdoc]: Lies in the future: now < exp, allowing a small clock skew.
// check nbf [except redirect-same-mdoc]: If present, lies in the past: now ≥ nbf.
// check iat [except redirect-same-mdoc]: If present, not in the future.
// check validityInfo.validFrom [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Lies in the past: now ≥ validFrom, allowing a small clock skew.
// check validityInfo.validUntil [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Lies in the future: now < validUntil, allowing a small clock skew.
// check validityInfo.signed [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Not in the future, and within the validity period of the signer certificate.

// ── Verifier checks the status list entry ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 7.8, dc-api 7.7, redirect-cross 7.8, redirect-same-mdoc 7.8
// Asks the issuer's Token Status List whether the credential was revoked or suspended. Only after the credential itself passed the checks above (Token Status List §8.3). What a non-valid status means for the transaction is Verifier policy (SD-JWT VC §3.4).
// rule: redirect-same, dc-api, redirect-cross: Verifier: SHOULD | redirect-same-mdoc: Verifier: MAY · "If status is present in the verified payload of the SD-JWT, the status SHOULD be checked. It depends on the Verifier policy to reject or accept a presentation of a SD-JWT VC based on the status of the Verifiable Credential." · "EAA-6.2.10.1-05: Verification of the MSO revocation list is optional for the wallet-relying party and where applicable, verification shall comply with the verification requirements specified in the token status list specification and the status structure specification set out in in EAA-6.2.10.1-01."
// check status.status_list.uri [except redirect-same-mdoc]: An https URL. The Verifier fetches the Status List Token from it (or uses a cached copy that has not expired).
// check Status List Token [except redirect-same-mdoc]: typ statuslist+jwt, signature valid and from the issuer (or a party it trusts for status), sub equals the uri, exp not passed.
// check status.status_list.idx [except redirect-same-mdoc]: The entry at idx in the decompressed list (bits per entry as the token's bits says) is 0x00 VALID. 0x01 INVALID: reject. 0x02 SUSPENDED: per policy, usually reject.
// check MSO status [only redirect-same-mdoc]: Optional (HAIP §5.3.1). If present, a map with status_list or identifier_list (CIR Annex IV EAA-6.2.10.1-17). Checking it is optional for the relying party; if it checks, it follows the Token Status List rules (EAA-6.2.10.1-05).
// check status.status_list.uri [only redirect-same-mdoc]: A URI. The Verifier fetches the Status List Token in CWT format from it (or uses a cached copy within its ttl).
// check Status List Token (CWT) [only redirect-same-mdoc]: COSE_Sign1 with type (16) application/statuslist+cwt and x5chain in the protected header; the chain leads to status_list.certificate if present, else to the CA that signed the MSO's x5chain (EAA-6.2.10.1-06). sub (2) equals the uri; exp (4) present and not passed.
// check status.status_list.idx [only redirect-same-mdoc]: The entry at idx (1 bit per entry, EAA-6.2.10.1-12.1) is 0 VALID. 1 INVALID: reject. For the PID no other value is used (EAA-6.2.10.1-03.1).
// check status.identifier_list [only redirect-same-mdoc]: If the MSO uses an identifier list instead: the CWT at its uri has type application/identifierlist+cwt, and the MSO is revoked if its id is among the identifiers (EAA-6.2.10.1-09, -10.1).

// ── Response endpoint answer to the Wallet ── Verifier → Wallet Unit · steps redirect-same 8, redirect-cross 8, redirect-same-mdoc 8
export interface ResponseAck {
  headers: {
    "Content-Type"?: string // no rule
    "Cache-Control"?: string // no rule · Caching directives; responses carrying tokens or nonces use no-store so intermediaries keep no copy.
  }
  body: {
    redirect_uri: string // except redirect-cross: MUST · Where the Wallet sends the user's browser after posting the response (same device); carries a response_code. · "Verifiers MUST include redirect_uri in the HTTP response to the Wallet's HTTP POST to the response_uri, as defined in Section 8.2 of [OIDF.OID4VP]."
  }
}

// ── Wallet opens the redirect_uri in the browser (same device) ── Wallet Unit → Verifier · steps redirect-same 9, redirect-same-mdoc 9
// rule: Wallet: MUST · "Wallets MUST follow the redirect to redirect_uri."

// ── Verifier frontend fetches the response with response_code (same device) ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same 10, redirect-same-mdoc 10

// ── navigator.credentials.get() request (Digital Credentials API) ── Verifier → Browser / OS · steps dc-api 2
// Argument of navigator.credentials.get(): the digital member lists one or more requests, each naming a protocol and carrying its data.
// rule: Verifier: MUST [R25]
export interface DcApiRequest {
  body: {
    digital: { // MUST · Argument of navigator.credentials.get(): the digital member lists one or more requests, each naming a protocol and carrying its data. [R25]
      requests: { // MUST [R25]
        protocol?: string // no rule · Presentation protocol identifier for the Digital Credentials API, e.g. openid4vp-v1-signed.
        data: { // MUST · The signed Request Object handed to the Wallet by the browser or operating system. [R26]
          request: RequestObject // MUST [R26]
        }
      }[]
    }
  }
}
// carries RequestObject (defined at Request Object (JAR))
// also:
//   - Support of the API-mediated mechanism (Digital Credentials API): Verifier: MUST; Wallet: MUST · "OIDFVP-HAIP-SUPPORT-05: A Relying Party shall meet the requirements specified in clauses 6.5 of the present Annex." · "OIDFVP-HAIP-SUPPORT-02: The EUDI Wallet shall meet the requirements specified in clause 6.5 of the present Annex."
//   - Accepting unsigned requests over the Digital Credentials API: Wallet: MUST NOT ⚠ · "OIDFVP-HAIP-API-03: The EUDI Wallet shall not support Authorization Requests with exchange protocol value set to "openid4vp-1-unsigned"."

// ── Proximity check in cross-device flows over the mediating API ── Browser / OS → Wallet Unit · steps dc-api 3
// rule: Wallet: MUST · "OIDFVP-HAIP-ADD-API-05: EUDI Wallets shall verify, in cross-device flows, using the mediating API, that the interacting device is in close physical proximity to the EUDI Wallet"

// ── Digital Credentials API response ── Wallet Unit → Browser / OS · steps dc-api 5
// What navigator.credentials.get() returns to the Verifier's web page: the protocol used and the Wallet's response data.
export interface DcApiResponse {
  body: {
    protocol?: string // no rule
    data: { // MUST · The encrypted Authorization Response returned through the Digital Credentials API. [R27]
      response: AuthorizationResponse // MUST [R27]
    }
  }
}
// carries KeyBindingJwt (defined at Authorization Response posted to response_uri (direct_post.jwt)), PresentedSdJwtVc (defined at Authorization Response posted to response_uri (direct_post.jwt)), AuthorizationResponse (defined at Authorization Response posted to response_uri (direct_post.jwt))

// ── Verifier frontend forwards the response to its backend (application-specific) ── Browser / OS → Verifier · steps dc-api 6

// ── Wallet support of the redirect mechanism for cross-device flows ── Verifier → Wallet (phone) · steps redirect-cross 2
// rule: Verifier: SHOULD NOT; Wallet: SHOULD NOT · "Verifiers are RECOMMENDED to use only the "same-device" flow unless the Verifier does not rely on session binding for phishing resistance" · "OIDFVP-HAIP-SUPPORT-03: The EUDI Wallet should not support the redirects-based mechanism specified in clause 6.4 for cross-device presentation flows."
export interface CrossDeviceRedirect {
  query: {
    client_id: string // MUST [R2]
    request_uri: string // MUST [R3]
  }
}

// ── Verifier frontend learns the result from its backend (application-specific) ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-cross 9

// ── Wallet signs the DeviceAuthentication over the SessionTranscript (mdoc) ── Wallet Unit → Wallet Unit · steps redirect-same-mdoc 5.4

// ── Verifier checks each presented data element against the MSO valueDigests ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same-mdoc 7.5
// The data elements travel outside the issuer's signature; the MSO it signed holds their digests. Each presented element must hash to its digest, so the issuer signature covers it (OpenID4VP §8.6, HAIP §8). Digests without a presented element are the elements the Wallet withheld.
// rule: Verifier: MUST [R24]
// check MSO digestAlgorithm: A hash algorithm the Verifier accepts, at least SHA-256 (HAIP §8).
// check IssuerSignedItemBytes: Each element is a tag-24 byte string decoding to {digestID, random, elementIdentifier, elementValue}.
// check digestID: Has an entry in MSO valueDigests under the element's namespace; no two presented elements of a namespace share one.
// check MSO valueDigests: The digestAlgorithm hash over the complete IssuerSignedItemBytes (tag 24 included) equals valueDigests[namespace][digestID]. Mismatch: reject.
// check elementValue: Has the CBOR type the namespace defines for elementIdentifier, e.g. full-date (tag 1004) for birth_date (CIR Annex I Table 6).

// ── Verifier rebuilds the SessionTranscript and verifies the deviceSignature ── Verifier → Verifier · steps redirect-same-mdoc 7.6
// The deviceSignature is the mdoc's holder binding: the device key the issuer put into the MSO signs the SessionTranscript, which carries client_id, nonce, the encryption key and response_uri of this request (OpenID4VP B.2.5, B.2.6, §14.1.2). The Verifier rebuilds it from its own Request Object; a presentation made for another request fails here.
// rule: Verifier: MUST · "Issuers, Verifiers, and Wallets MUST, at a minimum, support ECDSA with P-256 and SHA-256 (JOSE algorithm identifier ES256; COSE algorithm identifier -7 or -9, as applicable) for the purpose of validating the following: … the signature of the Verifiable Presentation, e.g., KB-JWT of an SD-JWT VC, or deviceSignature CBOR structure in case of ISO mdocs."
// check SessionTranscript: Rebuilt by the Verifier, not read from the response: [null, null, OpenID4VPHandover] (OpenID4VP B.2.6.1).
// check OpenID4VPHandover: ["OpenID4VPHandover", SHA-256 over the CBOR-encoded OpenID4VPHandoverInfo].
// check handover clientId: client_id of this transaction's Request Object, prefix included, e.g. x509_hash:<hash of the access certificate>.
// check handover nonce: nonce of this transaction's Request Object.
// check handover jwkThumbprint: RFC 7638 SHA-256 thumbprint of this transaction's ephemeral encryption key, as a byte string. Another key means the response was re-encrypted (OpenID4VP B.2.6.2).
// check handover responseUri: response_uri of this transaction's Request Object.
// check deviceAuth: Holds a deviceSignature (OpenID4VP B.2.5 also allows a MAC).
// check alg (deviceSignature header): At least -7 (ES256) supported (HAIP §7); one of vp_formats_supported.mso_mdoc.deviceauth_alg_values when the request set it (OpenID4VP B.2.2).
// check deviceSignature: Payload null (detached). Verifies over DeviceAuthenticationBytes, i.e. tag 24 over ["DeviceAuthentication", SessionTranscript, docType, DeviceNameSpacesBytes], with the MSO deviceKey.
// check MSO deviceKey: The COSE_Key under deviceKeyInfo of the MSO the issuer signed; its private key sits in the Wallet's WSCD (CIR Annex I 4.1).
// check deviceSigned.nameSpaces: An empty map: under the CIR a Wallet presents no device-signed data elements, except data the Verifier sent as transaction data (ISO/IEC 18013-5-RESP-02 NOTE 2).

// ── Role-wide obligations (all flows) ──
// - Credential Format Profile (HAIP §6) for the requested format: Verifier: MUST; Wallet: MUST · The HAIP §6 profile the presentation follows for its credential format: ISO mdocs for mso_mdoc, IETF SD-JWT VCs for dc+sd-jwt. · "OIDFVP-HAIP-GEN-04: When the format of the requested attestation complies with [10] Relying Parties and EUDI Wallets shall comply with the 'ISO mdocs' profile in [11] Section 6." · "OIDFVP-HAIP-GEN-05: When the format of the requested attestation complies with [2], Relying Parties and EUDI Wallets shall comply with the 'IETF SD-JWT VCs' profile in [11] Section 6."
// - ISO mdoc presentation (mso_mdoc): Verifier: MUST; Wallet: MUST · A presentation in ISO/IEC 18013-5 format: CBOR structures signed with COSE instead of JWTs. · "The Wallet and Verifier MUST support at least one of the following Credential Format Profiles defined in Section 6: IETF SD-JWT VC or ISO mdoc. Ecosystems SHOULD clearly indicate which of these formats, IETF SD-JWT VC, ISO mdoc, or both, are required to be supported."
// - DeviceResponse: no rule · CBOR structure the Wallet returns, base64url encoded as the vp_token value: the presented documents and a status code.
// - status.identifier_list: Verifier: MUST where the Verifier needs to verify the revocation status of the PID or an EAA; Issuer: MUST when the identifier list mechanism is used · Identifier list reference: the MSO's identifier (id), where the identifier list can be fetched (uri) and optionally a certificate. The MSO is revoked if its id is on that list. · "EAA-6.2.10.1-05.1: Where a wallet-relying party needs to be able to verify the revocation status of person identification data or electronic attestations of attributes, it shall support both the attestation status list mechanism and the attestation revocation list mechanism set out in EAA-6.2.10.1-02." · "EAA-6.2.10.1-01.1: When implementing the identifier list mechanism, the status element shall contain the identifier_list element as set out in EAA-6.2.10.1-11."
// - status_list / identifier_list certificate: Verifier: MUST where the certificate element is present and the Verifier checks the revocation status; Issuer: MAY · Optional certificate in the status reference whose key signed the top-level certificate of the revocation list's x5chain; the Verifier uses it as trust anchor for that chain. · "EAA-6.2.10.1-06.1.1: The wallet-relying party instance shall use that certificate as a trust anchor for the verification of the x5chain element in the MSO revocation list structure." · "EAA-6.2.10.1-06: The identifier_list and status_list in the MSO may contain the certificate element."
// - MSO revocation list (CWT): Issuer: MUST · The COSE_Sign1 CWT the status reference points to: a Status List Token (application/statuslist+cwt) or an identifier list (application/identifierlist+cwt). · "EAA-6.2.10.1-07: An MSO revocation list shall be implemented in accordance with the token status list specification as a status list token in CWT format." · "the CWT shall be a COSE_Sign1 object using one of the following signature algorithms for calculating the signature: (a) 'ES256' (ECDSA with curve NIST P-256 and SHA-256); … (f) 'ESB512' (ECDSA with curve brainpoolP512r1 and SHA-512);"
// - type (protected header, label 16): Issuer: MUST ⚠ · Media type of the revocation list CWT: application/statuslist+cwt for a status list, application/identifierlist+cwt for an identifier list. · "16 (type): REQUIRED. The type of the CWT MUST be application/ statuslist+cwt"
// - status list (65533) / identifier list (65530) claim: Issuer: MUST when the status list mechanism is used ⚠ · The list itself: a StatusList {bits, lst} under claim key 65533, or an IdentifierList {identifiers} under claim key 65530. · "EAA-6.2.10.1-12.1: The bits element in the StatusList structure shall be set to 1." · "EAA-6.2.10.1-03.2: For the identifier list, only revoked MSOs, as opposed to temporarily suspended MSOs, shall be put in the identifier list."
// - exp (claim 4): Issuer: MUST · Time after which the revocation list is considered expired by its issuer; a Verifier fetches a fresh copy after it. · "EAA-6.2.10.1-08: For the MSO revocation list for the identifier list and status list mechanism, the following requirements apply: — the exp claim shall be present."
// - x5chain (protected header, label 33): Verifier: MUST where the MSO's status reference has no certificate element and the Verifier checks the revocation status; Issuer: MUST where the MSO's status reference carries the certificate element · Certificate chain that verifies the revocation list's signature; it chains to the status reference's certificate or, without one, to the CA that signed the MSO's x5chain. · "EAA-6.2.10.1-06.2.1: The wallet-relying party instance shall use that certificate as a trust anchor for the verification of the x5chain element in the MSO revocation list structure." · "the CWT shall contain the x5chain in the protected header that contains the certificate or chain of certificates to verify the signature of the MSO revocation list." · "EAA-6.2.10.1-06.1: Where the certificate element is present, it shall contain a certificate containing the public key that signed or sealed the top-level certificate in the x5chain element in the MSO revocation list structure." · "EAA-6.2.10.1-06.2: Where the certificate element is not present, the top-level certificate in the x5chain element in the MSO revocation list structure shall be signed or sealed by the certificate used to sign the certificate in the x5chain element of the MSO."
// - SessionTranscript: no rule · [DeviceEngagementBytes, EReaderKeyBytes, Handover]: what the deviceSignature binds the presentation to. Over OpenID4VP the first two are null.
// - OpenID4VPHandover: no rule · ["OpenID4VPHandover", SHA-256 of the CBOR-encoded OpenID4VPHandoverInfo].
// - OpenID4VPHandoverInfo: no rule · [client_id, nonce, JWK thumbprint, response_uri] from the signed Request Object; its CBOR encoding is hashed into the handover.
// - handover clientId: no rule · client_id of the request, including the Client Identifier Prefix (x509_hash:).
// - handover nonce: no rule · nonce of the request; binds the presentation to this transaction.
// - handover jwkThumbprint: no rule · RFC 7638 SHA-256 thumbprint of the Verifier's response encryption key, as a byte string; null if the response is not encrypted.
// - handover responseUri: no rule · response_uri (or redirect_uri) of the request.
// - ISO/IEC 18013-7 Annex C over the Digital Credentials API (the CIR's ISO/IEC-mdoc profile): Verifier: SHOULD; Wallet: MUST · The other way to request an mdoc online: an ISO DeviceRequest through the Digital Credentials API instead of OpenID4VP. Not drawn as a flow (ISO/IEC 18013-7 is not in the corpus). · "ISO/IEC 18013-SUPPORT-04: A Relying Party should implement the profile defined in clause 5.4 of the present document." · "EAAP-API-GEN-01: The EUDI Wallet shall support a mediating API that supports both protocols defined in clause 5.2 of [11] and in Annex C of [16]."

// RULE WORDING
// [R1] "OIDFVP-HAIP-COMMON-REQ-RO-23: The leaf certificate mentioned in OpenID4VP section 5.9.3 for use with the x509_hash Client Identifier Prefix shall be a RP access certificate as specified in ETSI TS 119 475 [14]."
// [R2] "For signed requests, the Verifier MUST use, and the Wallet MUST accept the Client Identifier Prefix x509_hash as defined in Section 5.9.3 of [OIDF.OID4VP]."
// [R3] "Signed Authorization Requests MUST be used by utilizing JWT-Secured Authorization Request (JAR) [RFC9101] with the request_uri parameter."
// [R4] "The "alg" (algorithm) Header Parameter identifies the cryptographic algorithm used to secure the JWS."
// [R5] "Response encryption MUST be used by utilizing response mode direct_post.jwt, as defined in Section 8.3 of [OIDF.OID4VP]."
// [R6] "The Wallet MUST support the Response Mode dc_api.jwt. The Verifier MUST use the Response Mode dc_api.jwt."
